1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:03,443
A message can be simple. See you at five.

2
00:00:03,623 --> 00:00:07,386
But getting it from one person to another raises bigger questions.

3
00:00:07,566 --> 00:00:12,574
Who can read it? Who may contact you? What happens when a phone is offline?

4
00:00:14,254 --> 00:00:18,800
Imagine the message changing into an unreadable pattern before it leaves the phone.

5
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:24,246
It goes into a locked box. The intended recipient's device is designed to open it.

6
00:00:24,426 --> 00:00:26,884
This is our picture of end to end encryption.

7
00:00:28,564 --> 00:00:32,332
A courier carries the sealed box using delivery information.

8
00:00:32,512 --> 00:00:38,754
In today's prototype, a relay helps messages travel. It is still an infrastructure dependency.

9
00:00:38,934 --> 00:00:44,919
The encryption design aims to protect the content, while delivery information may remain visible.

10
00:00:46,599 --> 00:00:51,256
Your phone checks contact approval and blocking separately from the message's words.

11
00:00:51,436 --> 00:00:57,125
Calls, accepting files, and optional conversation history have their own choices.

12
00:00:57,305 --> 00:01:03,436
With invitation-only mode configured, the relay also checks identity-key possession and membership.

13
00:01:05,116 --> 00:01:09,834
Now imagine several couriers, each receiving instructions for the next leg.

14
00:01:10,014 --> 00:01:15,675
This illustrates a proposed routing idea to investigate, not today's completed service.

15
00:01:15,855 --> 00:01:21,458
A courier may still observe its immediate neighbors. We are not promising an invisible trail.

16
00:01:23,138 --> 00:01:28,242
If the recipient is offline, encrypted text can wait on a temporary shelf.

17
00:01:28,422 --> 00:01:33,480
That shelf has limits and lives in memory. A relay restart can lose waiting messages.

18
00:01:33,660 --> 00:01:36,825
It is a temporary queue, not a permanent archive.

19
00:01:38,505 --> 00:01:42,250
A box accepted by the relay is not a message read by a person.

20
00:01:42,430 --> 00:01:49,013
When a live send has an uncertain outcome, the client does not automatically create a fresh offline send.

21
00:01:49,193 --> 00:01:54,355
That avoids a blind second attempt. It is not a guarantee against every duplicate.

22
00:01:56,035 --> 00:02:00,395
At the recipient's device, the sealed message becomes readable again.

23
00:02:00,575 --> 00:02:07,790
Identity material has local vault protection. Keeping conversation history is a separate, optional choice.

24
00:02:07,970 --> 00:02:13,818
Trusted keys and safe devices still matter. A locked box cannot repair a compromised phone.

25
00:02:15,498 --> 00:02:21,225
Development corrected specific cancellation, cleanup, and identity-change races.

26
00:02:21,405 --> 00:02:26,351
One verified developer run passed seven hundred thirty-seven automated tests.

27
00:02:26,531 --> 00:02:35,546
An intermittent offline timeout and an invitation-bookkeeping conflict remain unresolved. Tests guide the work; they do not certify security.

28
00:02:37,226 --> 00:02:44,187
Our next research direction brings people, devices, and artificial intelligence assistants into this picture.

29
00:02:44,367 --> 00:02:49,400
An assistant might receive one task, with permission checked outside its own words.

30
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:55,091
That general permission layer is proposed research, not a capability already proven here.

31
00:02:56,771 --> 00:03:00,666
Alice Talk is a temporary name for an experimental project.

32
00:03:00,846 --> 00:03:04,512
The goal is useful communication with clearer human control.

33
00:03:04,692 --> 00:03:10,528
Independent review and broader real-device testing are still needed. Help us test the next step.
